Project Background Context:
Building operators and facilities managers need an automated diagnostic tool that can detect and identify faults in HVAC components such as chillers and cooling-tower subsystems. The tool should combine sensed operating data with equipment specifications and configuration information, then present diagnostic results that help users monitor equipment condition. Service technicians and energy-service personnel may also use it during maintenance or remote supervision.

Current Slots:
[
  {
    "name": "project_goals",
    "category": "goals",
    "value": "Help people spot problems early and quickly understand what part of the system is likely causing them so they can act before equipment performance degrades or a failure becomes obvious."
  },
  {
    "name": "stakeholder_roles",
    "category": "stakeholders",
    "value": "Building operators, facilities managers, service technicians, and energy-service personnel. Operators use it for routine monitoring and alarm triage; technicians use the same results to guide troubleshooting during site visits or remote diagnostics."
  },
  {
    "name": "functional_needs",
    "category": "functionality",
    "value": "Detect and identify high-value faults in chillers and cooling towers, including sensor issues, abnormal temperatures or pressures, poor heat transfer, valve or pump problems, fan or motor faults, and general efficiency losses. Provide a clear likely fault, supporting evidence, and a practical next step such as checking a sensor, inspecting a component, verifying setpoints, or scheduling maintenance."
  },
  {
    "name": "business_rules",
    "category": "business_logic",
    "value": "Use a layered approach to distinguish sensor problems from true equipment faults before assigning blame. If multiple faults explain the same symptoms, rank them by likelihood and impact and clearly indicate ambiguity rather than forcing a single answer. Treat repeated sensor anomalies as stronger evidence only when they persist over time and align with other operating data. Apply a confidence threshold so low-confidence results are flagged as warning or needs review, while higher-confidence results can produce specific diagnostic recommendations."
  },
  {
    "name": "data_entities",
    "category": "data",
    "value": "Core operating sensor points for chiller and cooling-tower subsystems, including temperatures, pressures, flow where available, valve positions, fan status, motor status, and power or current readings. Also include equipment specifications, control sequence/configuration, setpoints, operating mode, ambient conditions and weather when available, and asset metadata such as make, model, capacity, and overridden or out-of-service components."
  },
  {
    "name": "constraints",
    "category": "constraints",
    "value": null
  },
  {
    "name": "quality_concerns",
    "category": "quality",
    "value": "Compare current behavior against short-term history and a learned normal baseline, using recent trends over hours or days and similar operating conditions over a longer period. Update baselines carefully so bad behavior is not learned as normal, require sustained deviation rather than brief spikes, and flag drifting performance as an early warning below confirmed faults."
  },
  {
    "name": "boundary_conditions",
    "category": "exceptions",
    "value": "Diagnostic interpretation depends on operating mode, since the same readings mean different things when the unit is starting, running normally, or staged off. Results should account for overridden or out-of-service components."
  },
  {
    "name": "diagnostic_output_format",
    "category": "user_interface",
    "value": "Show a clear likely fault with the evidence behind it and a practical next step. When confidence is low, present a warning or needs-review state; when multiple faults fit, show ranked alternatives and indicate ambiguity."
  },
  {
    "name": "alert_and_triage_workflow",
    "category": "workflow",
    "value": "Operators use the tool for routine monitoring and alarm triage, while technicians use the results for troubleshooting during site visits or remote diagnostics."
  },
  {
    "name": "maintenance_action_guidance",
    "category": "functionality",
    "value": "Provide a clear likely fault, the evidence behind it, and a practical next step such as checking a sensor, inspecting a component, verifying setpoints, or scheduling maintenance."
  },
  {
    "name": "fault_priority_criteria",
    "category": "business_logic",
    "value": "Rank competing faults by likelihood and impact. Use confidence thresholds to determine whether to issue a specific diagnostic recommendation or a warning/needs-review state."
  },
  {
    "name": "equipment_scope_and_asset_hierarchy",
    "category": "data",
    "value": "Start with high-value faults in chillers and cooling-tower subsystems."
  },
  {
    "name": "diagnostic_input_data_sources",
    "category": "data",
    "value": "Use core operating sensor points plus equipment specifications, control sequence/configuration, setpoints, operating mode, ambient conditions and weather when available, and asset metadata."
  },
  {
    "name": "sensor_and_tag_normalization_rules",
    "category": "constraints",
    "value": null
  },
  {
    "name": "fault_model_update_and_learning_policy",
    "category": "business_logic",
    "value": "Compare current behavior against short-term history and a learned normal baseline using similar operating conditions. Update baselines carefully to avoid learning abnormal behavior, and require sustained deviation before declaring a fault. Use early-warning status for drift that is not yet a confirmed fault."
  },
  {
    "name": "integration_and_export_interfaces",
    "category": "workflow",
    "value": "Integrate with BMS or SCADA for live operating data and equipment status, with a historian for trends and historical data, and with CMMS or work-order systems so confirmed or high-priority diagnoses can become maintenance requests. Sync asset IDs, points, and configuration details, and avoid duplicate alert flooding."
  },
  {
    "name": "operational_environment_and_asset_context",
    "category": "data",
    "value": "Include equipment specifications, control sequence or configuration, setpoints, operating mode, ambient conditions and weather when available, and basic asset metadata such as make, model, capacity, and whether components have been overridden or taken out of service."
  },
  {
    "name": "mode_aware_diagnostic_logic",
    "category": "business_logic",
    "value": "Recognize startup, normal run, standby, staging, shutdown, maintenance, and test mode. Interpret readings differently depending on the operating state, since some symptoms may be expected during transitions and only indicate a fault if they persist beyond the normal transition period."
  },
  {
    "name": "override_and_out_of_service_handling",
    "category": "exceptions",
    "value": "If a component is bypassed, manually overridden, or marked out of service, do not rank faults on that component as strongly. Note those conditions clearly and lower confidence across related faults because the affected data may not be reliable for normal performance diagnosis."
  },
  {
    "name": "operating_state_classification",
    "category": "functionality",
    "value": "Classify the asset into main operating states such as startup, normal run, standby, staging, shutdown, maintenance, and test mode so diagnostic interpretation can adjust to the current state."
  },
  {
    "name": "diagnostic_explanation_traceability",
    "category": "quality",
    "value": "Show traceability back to the data and rules used for the diagnosis. Operators should see a short practical summary with suspected fault, confidence, and immediate action; technicians should see supporting evidence, key sensor trends, triggered rules or patterns, and related conditions; facilities managers should see an intermediate level focused on equipment condition, risk, and whether escalation or scheduling is needed."
  },
  {
    "name": "alert_delivery_and_notification_channels",
    "category": "workflow",
    "value": "Push only meaningful notifications and avoid flooding operators with duplicate alerts."
  },
  {
    "name": "historical_trend_and_baseline_comparison",
    "category": "functionality",
    "value": "Compare current behavior against short-term history and a learned normal baseline, using recent trends over hours or days and similar operating conditions over a longer period. Require sustained deviation rather than a brief spike, and flag drifting performance as an early warning when it is not yet a confirmed fault."
  },
  {
    "name": "recommendation_severity_and_escalation_policy",
    "category": "business_logic",
    "value": "Use a confidence threshold to separate warning or needs-review results from specific diagnostic recommendations. Rank ambiguous multi-fault cases by likelihood and impact, and treat early drift as a lower-severity warning below confirmed faults."
  },
  {
    "name": "system_response_and_update_timing",
    "category": "constraints",
    "value": null
  },
  {
    "name": "explanation_detail_and_audience_tuning",
    "category": "user_interface",
    "value": "Tailor explanation depth by role: operators get a short, practical summary with suspected fault, confidence, and immediate action; technicians get supporting evidence, trends, triggered rules or patterns, and related conditions; facilities managers get an intermediate level focused on condition, risk, and scheduling or escalation."
  },
  {
    "name": "data_quality_and_missing_point_handling",
    "category": "constraints",
    "value": null
  },
  {
    "name": "deployment_and_integration_environment",
    "category": "workflow",
    "value": null
  },
  {
    "name": "notification_and_escalation_rules",
    "category": "workflow",
    "value": null
  },
  {
    "name": "user_role_access_control",
    "category": "constraints",
    "value": "Access should vary by role. Operators can view live diagnostics, summary explanations, and acknowledge or comment on issues, but cannot change core rules or broadly export sensitive data. Facilities managers have broader view and reporting access, including trends and historical summaries. Technicians can see deeper diagnostics and export detailed evidence for troubleshooting. Control functions such as changing thresholds or diagnostic settings are limited to authorized admin or engineering users. Remote service or energy-service staff should only access assigned assets, with export rights controlled by policy."
  },
  {
    "name": "diagnostic_audit_trail_retention",
    "category": "quality",
    "value": null
  },
  {
    "name": "explanation_trace_elements",
    "category": "functionality",
    "value": "Include the supporting evidence, key sensor trends, triggered rules or patterns, related conditions, confidence, and immediate action or recommendation, with depth adjusted by user role."
  },
  {
    "name": "cmms_bms_integration_points",
    "category": "workflow",
    "value": "Integrate with BMS or SCADA for live operating data and equipment status, with a historian for trends and historical data, and with CMMS or work-order systems so confirmed or high-priority diagnoses can become maintenance requests. Sync asset IDs, points, and configuration details."
  },
  {
    "name": "alert_routing_and_acknowledgement_flow",
    "category": "workflow",
    "value": "Operators should be able to acknowledge or comment on issues, and alerts should be routed in a way that avoids duplicate flooding."
  },
  {
    "name": "audit_trail_and_action_history_retention",
    "category": "quality",
    "value": null
  },
  {
    "name": "export_scope_and_sensitive_data_controls",
    "category": "constraints",
    "value": "Operators should not export sensitive data broadly. Technicians can export detailed evidence for troubleshooting. Remote service or energy-service staff should only access assigned assets, with export rights controlled by policy."
  }
]

Previous Abductions:
[
  {
    "surprising_fact": "The tool must interpret the same readings differently depending on operating mode, and also account for overridden or out-of-service components when diagnosing faults.",
    "suspected_reason": "The HVAC system likely has dynamic control states and temporary maintenance bypasses that can make normal sensor patterns look faulty. The diagnostic logic therefore needs explicit mode- and configuration-awareness to avoid false positives and misattributing symptoms to components that are intentionally disabled or overridden.",
    "new_slot": "mode_aware_diagnostic_logic"
  },
  {
    "surprising_fact": "The tool needs to explain diagnoses differently for operators, technicians, and facilities managers, with traceability back to the data and rules used.",
    "suspected_reason": "Different user groups likely make different operational decisions from the same diagnosis, so the system needs role-specific explanations to support fast triage for operators, field troubleshooting for technicians, and risk/escalation decisions for managers while maintaining trust and auditability.",
    "new_slot": "user_role_access_control"
  },
  {
    "surprising_fact": "The diagnostic tool is expected to use live diagnostics, acknowledgements/comments, detailed evidence export, and role-based access control, but the required integrations, notification path, and audit/retention behavior are still unspecified.",
    "suspected_reason": "This suggests the system is intended to fit into an operational workflow rather than just provide standalone analytics. That usually means it must integrate with existing BMS/CMMS or monitoring tools, support alert distribution to the right people, and preserve an audit trail for accountability, troubleshooting, and compliance.",
    "new_slot": "cmms_bms_integration_points"
  }
]

Dialogue History:
Assistant: What are the primary outcomes you want this diagnostic tool to achieve for operators and technicians—for example, which HVAC faults should it detect first, what diagnostic actions or recommendations should it provide, and how should users use those results in their daily monitoring or maintenance work?
User: The main outcome is to help people spot problems early and quickly understand what part of the system is likely causing them, so they can act before the equipment performance degrades or a failure becomes obvious. I’d expect it to start with the high-value faults in chillers and cooling towers, like sensor issues, abnormal temperatures or pressures, poor heat transfer, valve or pump problems, fan or motor faults, and general efficiency losses.
For recommendations, it should give a clear likely fault, the evidence behind it, and a practical next step, like checking a sensor, inspecting a component, verifying setpoints, or scheduling maintenance. In daily use, operators would use it for routine monitoring and alarm triage, while technicians would use the same results to guide troubleshooting during site visits or remote diagnostics.
Assistant: What rules or decision logic should the diagnostic tool follow when identifying and ranking faults—for example, how should it handle overlapping symptoms, confidence thresholds, repeated sensor anomalies, or cases where multiple possible faults could explain the same evidence?
User: It should use a layered approach so it can separate likely sensor problems from real equipment faults before it assigns blame to a component. If several faults fit the same symptoms, I’d want it to rank them by likelihood and impact, and clearly show when the result is ambiguous rather than forcing a single answer.
Repeated sensor anomalies should probably be treated as a stronger signal only if they persist over time and line up with other operating data, otherwise the tool should flag them as possibly intermittent instrumentation issues. For confidence, I’d expect a threshold below which it just raises a warning or “needs review” state, and above that it can make a more specific diagnostic recommendation.
Assistant: What specific input data sources and asset/context information should the diagnostic tool use—for example, which sensor points, equipment specifications, configuration details, operating modes, and site or environmental conditions are required to support reliable fault detection?
User: It should use the core operating sensor points for the chiller and cooling-tower subsystems, like temperatures, pressures, flow where available, valve positions, fan status, motor status, and power or current readings if the site has them. On the context side, it needs the equipment specifications, control sequence or configuration, setpoints, and what operating mode the asset is in, because the same readings mean different things depending on whether the unit is starting, running normally, or being staged off.
If available, ambient conditions and weather can help a lot too, especially for cooling-tower performance, and I’d also want basic asset metadata like make, model, capacity, and whether any components have been overridden or taken out of service.
Assistant: How should the diagnostic logic change its interpretation and fault ranking across different operating modes and temporary overrides—for example, what specific modes or states should it recognize, and how should it treat readings from components that are bypassed, overridden, or out of service?
User: It should recognize the main operating states that change the meaning of the readings, especially startup, normal run, standby, staging, shutdown, and any maintenance or test mode. In those cases, the same symptom may be expected or may indicate a fault only if it lasts beyond the normal transition period.
If a component is bypassed, manually overridden, or marked out of service, the tool should not rank faults on that component as strongly, because the data is no longer reliable for diagnosing normal performance. I’d still want it to note those conditions clearly and maybe lower confidence across related faults, since one overridden piece can affect the rest of the system’s behavior.
Assistant: Should the diagnostic tool compare current readings against historical trends or learned baselines, and if so, what time horizon, comparison method, and baseline rules should it use to decide when performance has drifted enough to indicate a fault?
User: Yes, it should compare current behavior against both short-term history and a learned normal baseline, because a lot of HVAC issues show up as gradual drift before they turn into a hard fault. I’d expect it to look at recent trends over hours or days for immediate changes, and also compare against similar operating conditions over a longer period, like the same mode, load range, and weather band.
The baseline should be updated carefully so it doesn’t learn bad behavior as normal, and it should probably require a sustained deviation, not just a brief spike, before declaring a fault. If performance is drifting but not yet clearly broken, I’d want it to flag that as an early warning and rank it below a confirmed fault.
Assistant: When the tool presents a diagnosis, how should it explain its reasoning and tailor the level of detail for different users—for example, what evidence or traceability should be shown to support the fault conclusion, and should operators, facilities managers, and technicians see different explanation styles or depths?
User: Yes, the explanation should be different by user role. Operators need a short, practical summary with the suspected fault, confidence, and immediate action, while technicians should see the supporting evidence, the key sensor trends, the rule or pattern that triggered it, and any related conditions that help them verify it in the field.
Facilities managers would probably want something in between, more focused on equipment condition, risk, and whether it needs scheduling or escalation. I’d also want the tool to show traceability back to the data it used, so users can see why it made the call instead of just seeing a black-box label.
Assistant: Should access to the diagnostic results, explanations, and any control or export functions vary by user role, and if so, what permissions should operators, facilities managers, technicians, and any other roles have?
User: Yes, access should vary by role. Operators should be able to view live diagnostics, explanations at a summary level, and acknowledge or comment on issues, but not change core rules or export sensitive data broadly. Facilities managers should have broader view and reporting access, including trends and historical summaries, while technicians should be able to see deeper diagnostics and export detailed evidence for troubleshooting.
Any control functions, like changing thresholds or diagnostic settings, should be limited to authorized admin or engineering users. If we have remote service or energy-service staff, they should probably get access only to the assets they are assigned to, with export rights controlled by policy.
Assistant: Which existing systems should this diagnostic tool integrate with for day-to-day operations—such as BMS/SCADA, CMMS, alarms, work-order systems, or historian platforms—and what should each integration be used for (for example, reading data, pushing alerts, creating work orders, or syncing equipment records)?
User: It should definitely integrate with the BMS or SCADA system for live operating data and basic equipment status, and with the historian if one exists for trends and older data. It should also connect to the CMMS or work-order system so confirmed issues or high-priority diagnoses can be turned into maintenance requests instead of just sitting as alerts.
For alarms, I’d want it to push only meaningful notifications, not flood the operators with duplicates, and for equipment records it should sync asset IDs, points, and configuration details so the diagnostics stay aligned with what’s actually installed.

